# Agent rules for this manuscript project

Project: {title} ({kind_label}). Author: {author_name}, file initials {initials}. Target journal:
`target_journal` in `PROJECT_INDEX.json`; its short code for file names is `venue` in
`manuscript.json` ({venue} when the project was set up; `msw.py retarget` changes both).
Tracked changes carry the author name **{revision_name}** (`author.revision_name` in
`manuscript.json`) and no other name; change it only when the author asks. Spelling: {spelling}.

These rules override general habits. The tools are the scientific-manuscript skill's `msw.py`:
`msw.py <command>` below means `python {msw} <command>`, run from this folder
({msw_note}; `--help` lists the commands). `msw.py status` shows the current file, author saves,
lock files, passes in flight and open items.

## Never delete, never overwrite

- Nothing in this folder is ever deleted. No `rm`, `del`, `Remove-Item`, `rmdir`, `unlink`,
  "move" done as copy-then-delete, or emptying a recycle bin.
- Never save over manuscripts, data, figures or records: Word files, anything in
  `01_Manuscripts/` and `03_Data/`, figure outputs and build scripts, proofs, the indexes and
  `06_Project_Docs/LEDGER.jsonl`. Tools write new files and refuse existing ones; the indexes and
  the generated blocks in `README.md` and `STATUS.md` change only through `msw.py`.
- The working files the workflow asks you to fill in are edited in place: the pass folder's
  `edits.json`, `VNN_CHANGES.md` and the `figures_changed` entry of its `pass.json` (until the
  pass is released), the configuration in `manuscript.json`, `06_Project_Docs/DECISIONS.md` (add
  entries; correct an old one below it, never rewrite it), the journal profile in
  `05_References/Journal_Guidelines/`, and, after a retarget, the lines of this file and of
  `README.md` that `msw.py retarget` reports as still naming the old journal. Once released, their
  copies in `01_Manuscripts/Versions/VNN/` are records like any other.
- To take a file out of view, move it: `msw.py archive plan --reason <Name> <paths>`, show the plan,
  then `msw.py archive apply ...`. Files go to `99_Archive/YYYY-MM-DD/<Name>/` under their original
  relative path, by rename, with the SHA-256 before and after recorded in
  `06_Project_Docs/LEDGER.jsonl`. Anything an index names cannot be archived.
- Never run `git clean`, `git checkout` (any form, including a branch, a commit or a path),
  `git switch`, `git restore`, `git reset --hard`, `git stash` or `git rm` here: they delete or
  overwrite working files. To move a branch pointer without touching files use `git branch -f`.
  "Ignored by git" means "not versioned", never "deleted".
- New versions only. Every change produces a new file with the next version number; released
  files, incoming files and the author's own saves are never edited or replaced. Tools write to new
  files or new folders and refuse existing ones.
- Records keep no machine paths: the indexes and the ledger hold paths relative to this folder, and
  a file taken in from elsewhere is recorded by its name and SHA-256 only.

## Word files and EndNote

- Change a manuscript only with `msw.py build <source> <edits.json> --out <new file>`. It writes
  every change as a minimal tracked change under the name above and gates the result. Never edit
  `document.xml` by hand or rewrite it with a general XML or docx library.
- EndNote citation fields, the bibliography, pictures and other fields are atoms: text may change
  around them, never inside them, and a citation stays in its clause.
- Do not refresh fields. The author refreshes EndNote, then the table of contents, then page
  numbers in Word, and says so when done.
- Writing fixes are tracked edits. Questions about the science, numbers, statistics or the
  direction of a claim follow `policy.science_changes` in `manuscript.json`: `comments` (the
  default) makes them Word comments for the author; `tracked_for_review` allows tracked edits the
  author reviews. Numbers change only with the author's approval or a recomputation from the data.
- Never drive the author's running Word. A `~$` lock file (Word) or a `.~lock.<name>#` file
  (LibreOffice) beside a document means it is open: do not move it or build from it until the
  author closes it.
- When the author saves the package's current copy in Word, register it with
  `msw.py author-save <file>`. The saved copy becomes the baseline for the next pass. A changed
  baseline or other older copy is not the current file: keep it with
  `msw.py intake <file> --role incoming --as-version <its version>`.
- A tracked copy returned by a supervisor or co-author that the next pass builds on:
  `msw.py intake "<returned file>" --role author_saved --state "<name> comments" --set-current`.
  It keeps the version number, carries the sender in its file name and records `returned_by`.
  Files kept only for reference go in with `--role incoming`.

