## Versions and names

- File names: `YYYY-MM-DD {initials} <venue> VNN <slug> <state>.docx`, where `<venue>` is the
  venue code in `manuscript.json`. States: `baseline` (the project's first file), `tracked
  verified` (released), `author accepted` or `author saved` (the author's own saves), `<name>
  comments` (a supervisor's or co-author's return), `accepted` (clean copy). A build inside its pass
  folder has the short name `VNN tracked.docx`; `release` gives the released copy its full name.
- Version numbers are integers, never reused, and keep counting across journal retargets
  (`msw.py retarget --venue <code> --target-journal "<name>" --journal-profile <profile>`). Claim
  one with `msw.py pass new --slug "<two to six words>"`; parallel sessions get different numbers.
- Before editing, run `msw.py status` and build on the newest file, which is the author's latest
  save when there is one.
- A command that stops part-way (a locked file, a crash) keeps a pending event in the ledger.
  `msw.py status` lists it; run the same command again and it finishes from that event.

## Release ritual (one pass makes one version)

1. `msw.py status`: note the current file; register any author save first.
2. `msw.py pass new --slug "<words>"`: claims VNN and creates the pass folder with `pass.json`,
   `edits.json` and `VNN_CHANGES.md`. Keep slugs short: file paths on Windows stop at 260
   characters.
3. Write the edits, then run the build command the pass printed (it writes
   `build01/VNN tracked.docx`). The build gates itself and writes only on PASS.
4. Render the accepted view of the build you will release into a new folder under the pass
   `proofs/` folder (`msw.py proof <build> --out <pass folder>/proofs/p1`) and look at it. A rebuilt
   pass needs a new proof: release refuses a proof whose `proof.json` names another build. Run
   native Word QA when available.
5. Independent review: a separate reviewer reads the artifacts themselves (the docx, its accepted
   and rejected views, the proof), not a summary. Fix and rebuild on FAIL.
6. Complete `VNN_CHANGES.md`: source, each change, Verification, open items, Not done. Record
   items that stay open across versions with `msw.py outstanding add --text "..."` and close them
   with `msw.py outstanding done --id O3`.
7. `msw.py release plan --pass <pass folder>`, then `msw.py release apply --pass <pass folder>`.
   It copies the build to `Versions/VNN/` under its full name
   (`YYYY-MM-DD {initials} <venue> VNN <slug> tracked verified.docx`), updates the
   current-deliverable folder (current plus baseline; older copies are moved to the archive after
   a twin check; figure versions named with `--figures`, or promoted and shown by the build, are
   copied into `Figures/`), the indexes, the generated blocks and the ledger.
8. Commit with the command `release apply` prints, after the author agrees.

## Before presenting a version

Present a version only after the gate reports PASS, the accepted view has been rendered and looked
at, and an independent reviewer has read the artifacts. Report: the new file's path; one line of
check results (gate, citations, reject view equals source, proof, reviewer); the steps left for the
author in Word (EndNote, then table of contents, then page numbers); numbered decisions, each with a
recommended option.
